1
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:07,679
Last time, we asked:

2
00:00:07,699 --> 00:00:09,279
A frog and a mosquito!

3
00:00:09,539 --> 00:00:12,959
Both lay their eggs in water, and both have young that look

4
00:00:12,979 --> 00:00:14,419
nothing like the adult.

5
00:00:14,689 --> 00:00:16,539
Are their life cycles the same?

6
00:00:16,586 --> 00:00:17,696
One, yes.

7
00:00:18,916 --> 00:00:19,796
Two, no.

8
00:00:21,274 --> 00:00:22,784
Hold on to your answer!

9
00:00:24,656 --> 00:00:27,336
A little cicada is hiding in this video.

10
00:00:27,586 --> 00:00:28,826
Can you spot it?

11
00:00:28,949 --> 00:00:31,319
Cicadas sing loudly, but they hide well.

12
00:00:31,499 --> 00:00:32,919
Look on the tree trunks!

13
00:00:32,971 --> 00:00:34,431
You against the Buddies!

14
00:00:34,701 --> 00:00:36,551
Lock in first with your fingers.

15
00:00:36,821 --> 00:00:38,651
One finger for every point.

16
00:00:39,232 --> 00:00:40,532
Today you'll learn:

17
00:00:40,737 --> 00:00:42,067
How to compare.

18
00:00:42,263 --> 00:00:43,703
Count the stages.

19
00:00:43,908 --> 00:00:45,138
Look at the young.

20
00:00:45,643 --> 00:00:46,583
Are you ready?

21
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:47,793
Three!

22
00:00:48,173 --> 00:00:48,833
Two!

23
00:00:49,203 --> 00:00:49,853
One!

24
00:00:50,273 --> 00:00:50,993
Go!

25
00:00:52,382 --> 00:00:53,272
Round one!

26
00:00:53,572 --> 00:00:55,392
Are their life cycles the same?

27
00:00:55,692 --> 00:00:57,002
Show me your fingers!

28
00:01:00,469 --> 00:01:01,449
Locked in!

29
00:01:01,577 --> 00:01:02,297
One!

30
00:01:02,427 --> 00:01:05,907
They both lay eggs in water, and both babies look nothing like

31
00:01:05,927 --> 00:01:06,897
their parents.

32
00:01:06,957 --> 00:01:08,357
So they are the same!

33
00:01:08,407 --> 00:01:09,037
Two!

34
00:01:09,197 --> 00:01:12,157
A frog is big and green, and a mosquito is tiny and

35
00:01:12,177 --> 00:01:12,817
buzzy.

36
00:01:13,047 --> 00:01:14,507
They are totally different!

37
00:01:14,557 --> 00:01:17,857
Two. I counted the stages on our board.

38
00:01:18,077 --> 00:01:21,027
The frog has three, but the mosquito has four.

39
00:01:21,303 --> 00:01:23,413
But I found two things that are the same!

40
00:01:23,493 --> 00:01:25,823
How can their life cycles be different?

41
00:01:26,471 --> 00:01:27,621
Look closer.

42
00:01:28,201 --> 00:01:31,121
When we compare, we look for what is the same, and what

43
00:01:31,141 --> 00:01:32,062
is different.

44
00:01:32,082 --> 00:01:34,602
What is the same are similarities.

45
00:01:35,172 --> 00:01:37,472
What is different are differences.

46
00:01:38,502 --> 00:01:42,002
Similarities are the things both life cycles have in common.

47
00:01:42,232 --> 00:01:44,619
Differences are the ways they are not the same.

48
00:01:44,639 --> 00:01:47,019
Momo found two real similarities.

49
00:01:47,579 --> 00:01:51,479
Both lay their eggs in water, and in both, the young do

50
00:01:51,499 --> 00:01:53,005
not look like the adult.

51
00:01:53,025 --> 00:01:54,625
But is there a difference?

52
00:01:55,195 --> 00:01:56,825
Let's count the stages.

53
00:01:59,465 --> 00:02:00,605
Be a raindrop!

54
00:02:00,905 --> 00:02:01,905
Shrink down.

55
00:02:02,175 --> 00:02:03,115
Quietly.

56
00:02:05,397 --> 00:02:09,057
You fall into the garden pond, beside some frog eggs.

57
00:02:09,837 --> 00:02:11,057
Days go by.

58
00:02:11,887 --> 00:02:13,887
Out wriggles a tadpole, the young.

59
00:02:14,396 --> 00:02:17,166
It grows legs, and its tail shrinks away.

60
00:02:17,956 --> 00:02:19,336
Out hops a frog.

61
00:02:20,156 --> 00:02:22,546
Egg, young, adult: three stages.

62
00:02:23,069 --> 00:02:24,019
Splash!

63
00:02:24,799 --> 00:02:28,989
You land in a flower-pot plate, beside tiny mosquito eggs.

64
00:02:29,759 --> 00:02:31,839
Out come the wrigglers, the larvae.

65
00:02:32,362 --> 00:02:35,392
Each one curls into a pupa, one more stage!

66
00:02:36,182 --> 00:02:38,032
Then out comes a mosquito.

67
00:02:38,912 --> 00:02:41,992
Egg, larva, pupa, adult: four stages.

68
00:02:43,653 --> 00:02:46,173
The answer is two: no, they are different!

69
00:02:46,453 --> 00:02:47,483
Two fingers?

70
00:02:47,753 --> 00:02:48,919
One finger up!

71
00:02:48,939 --> 00:02:52,319
The frog has three stages, and the mosquito has four.

72
00:02:52,459 --> 00:02:55,554
The mosquito has a pupa, and the frog does not.

73
00:02:55,574 --> 00:02:58,874
Basil picked two, but size does not tell us about a life

74
00:02:58,894 --> 00:02:59,594
cycle.

75
00:02:59,864 --> 00:03:01,639
So only Pip scores a point.

76
00:03:01,659 --> 00:03:03,929
We can see the stages, and count them.

77
00:03:04,219 --> 00:03:06,519
They are observable characteristics.

78
00:03:07,041 --> 00:03:09,661
Observable means we can see it, or count it.

79
00:03:09,811 --> 00:03:11,741
The number of stages is one.

80
00:03:11,961 --> 00:03:13,971
What the young looks like is another!

81
00:03:14,174 --> 00:03:15,414
Say it with me!

82
00:03:16,121 --> 00:03:19,781
The life cycles of the frog and the mosquito are similar because

83
00:03:19,801 --> 00:03:22,001
the young do not look like the adults.

84
00:03:22,491 --> 00:03:26,451
However, the life cycles are different because the frog has three stages

85
00:03:26,471 --> 00:03:29,901
in its life cycle, while the mosquito has four stages in its

86
00:03:29,921 --> 00:03:30,951
life cycle.

87
00:03:33,579 --> 00:03:34,669
My turn!

88
00:03:34,879 --> 00:03:37,969
The frog and the grasshopper both have three stages.

89
00:03:38,159 --> 00:03:40,959
So their life cycles are exactly the same!

90
00:03:41,084 --> 00:03:42,644
Something is not right.

91
00:03:42,914 --> 00:03:45,094
If you can spot it, cross your arms.

92
00:03:45,374 --> 00:03:46,334
Hold on!

93
00:03:51,747 --> 00:03:52,877
Arms crossed?

94
00:03:53,147 --> 00:03:54,357
One finger up!

95
00:03:54,409 --> 00:03:57,239
The number of stages is the same, but you forgot to look

96
00:03:57,259 --> 00:03:58,209
at the young!

97
00:03:59,006 --> 00:04:01,641
Look closer at the young, beside their parents.

98
00:04:01,661 --> 00:04:04,501
The tadpole has a tail and no legs, and it lives in

99
00:04:04,521 --> 00:04:05,161
water.

100
00:04:05,181 --> 00:04:06,902
It looks nothing like a frog!

101
00:04:06,922 --> 00:04:09,842
But the nymph has six legs and two feelers, just like a

102
00:04:09,862 --> 00:04:10,912
grasshopper.

103
00:04:11,162 --> 00:04:12,768
It only has no wings yet!

104
00:04:12,788 --> 00:04:15,858
One thing is the same, and one thing is different.

105
00:04:16,488 --> 00:04:17,918
Always look at both!

106
00:04:20,625 --> 00:04:21,915
Say it with me!

107
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:27,420
When we compare, we can use observable characteristics to identify similarities and

108
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:30,420
differences between the life cycles of the animals.

109
00:04:32,894 --> 00:04:34,354
Show me your score hand!

110
00:04:34,634 --> 00:04:36,024
One finger or more?

111
00:04:36,344 --> 00:04:37,704
You beat the Buddies.

112
00:04:37,974 --> 00:04:39,004
Two fingers?

113
00:04:39,284 --> 00:04:40,294
High five!

114
00:04:40,866 --> 00:04:42,456
Did you spot the cicada?

115
00:04:42,746 --> 00:04:44,876
It was on the leg of our board's easel!

116
00:04:45,011 --> 00:04:46,321
Here is a fun fact!

117
00:04:46,381 --> 00:04:49,171
Cicadas have three stages, like grasshoppers.

118
00:04:49,191 --> 00:04:53,701
But in America, some cicada nymphs live underground for seventeen years!

119
00:04:53,824 --> 00:04:55,104
Tell someone at home!

120
00:04:55,833 --> 00:04:58,033
Let's read today's key words together!

121
00:04:58,323 --> 00:04:59,083
Ready?

122
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:00,357
Similarities.

123
00:05:02,155 --> 00:05:03,019
Differences.

124
00:05:04,706 --> 00:05:06,596
Observable characteristics.

125
00:05:08,918 --> 00:05:09,858
Well done!

126
00:05:10,655 --> 00:05:12,435
Look, the hen is sitting on her eggs!

127
00:05:12,555 --> 00:05:14,926
So which came first, the chicken or the egg?

128
00:05:14,946 --> 00:05:16,536
Find out in Part Two!

129
00:05:17,696 --> 00:05:18,797
Say it with us!

130
00:05:19,597 --> 00:05:20,347
Ready?

131
00:05:20,470 --> 00:05:21,130
One!

132
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:22,170
two!

133
00:05:22,475 --> 00:05:23,650
Now you know!
